<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chp2sec2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

        <title>First Algebraic Properties</title>
        <p>
            In this investigation, you will deduces basic algebraic properties of vector spaces.
            Throughout, <m>V</m> will be taken to be a vector space over a field <m>F</m>.
        </p>
        <proposition>
            <title>The zero vector is unique</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>F</m> is a field, <m>V</m> is a vector space over <m>F</m>, and <m>\vect{0},\vect{0}'\in V</m> both satisfy the property that, for all <m>\vect{v}\in V</m>, <m>\vect{0}+\vect{v}=\vect{v}</m> and <m>\vect{0}'+\vect{v}=\vect{v}</m>, then <m>\vect{0}=\vect{0}'</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Try a direct proof.
                Carefully and appropriately utilize copy and paste.
                You may also find <xref ref="prop-field-addititive_identity_uniqueness"/> and <xref ref="def-vectorspace"/> useful.
            </p>
        </proof>
        <p>
            We are therefore justified in discussing <em>the</em> zero vector.
        </p>
        <proposition>
            <title>Inverses are unique</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>F</m> is a field, <m>V</m> is a vector space over <m>F</m>, <m>\vect{v}\in V</m>, and both <m>\vect{w}</m> and <m>\vect{w}'</m> satisfy the property that <m>\vect{v}+\vect{w}=\vect{0}</m> and <m>\vect{v}+\vect{w}'=\vect{0}</m>, then <m>\vect{w}=\vect{w}'</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Have you seen a similar result whose proof you may leverage?
            </p>
        </proof>

        <p> 
        We are therefore justified in discussing <em>the</em> additive inverse of a vector. Given a vector <m>\vect{v}\in V</m>, we will denote its additive inverse by <m>-\vect{v}</m>.
        </p>

        <proposition xml:id="prop-vs-zero_scaling">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>\vect{v}\in V</m>, then <m>0\vect{v} = \vect{0}</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Have you seen a similar result whose proof you may leverage?
            </p>
        </proof>
        <proposition xml:id="prop-vector_space_inverses_scaling">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>\vect{v}\in V</m>, then <m>(-1)\vect{v} = -\vect{v}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Have you seen a similar result whose proof you may leverage?
            </p>
        </proof>
        <proposition xml:id="prop-vs-scaling_zero_vector">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>k\in F</m>, then <m>k\vect{0}=\vect{0}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Have you seen a similar result whose proof you may leverage?
            </p>
        </proof>
    
</section>








